A||||10||Richard Parnell||Drivetrain||||9||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||10-06-1999||06:26 PM||||My 87 SC 4 banger w/ 5 spd has a problem with something worn somewhere in the drivetrain.  When I totally let up on the gas and then slightly (or even not so slightly) depress the gas it sort of clunks or jerks.  Somewhere something is worn.  Question is - bearings where axle meets wheel, bearing where axle meets tranny or something worn inside trans?  Any suggestions?||||reg||9
Z||000001||ChadMan||10-06-1999||06:59 PM||||My guess would be a motor/transaxle mount, and the most likely candidate would be the one at the front of the transaxle.  If it's bad, fix it quick, or it'll make a mess of the other mounts including the dog bone.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RZURCTN||10-06-1999||07:20 PM||||you can check out the thread on "shifting problems" by toolowfiero.  A lot of good info on clunking noise in that one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-06-1999||08:15 PM||||check the motor/tranny mounts and the dog bone.<P>A bad dog bone can allow the exhaust and/or air cleaner to strike the body of the car. Neither is a good thing. A worn stock dog bone can allow 2 or more inches of travel in the block.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-06-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||10-06-1999||11:10 PM||||I know I probably have some loose areas in the mounts and such, but I'm pretty sure this is in the wheel axle area.  it's a lot like bad u-joints in an old rear whell drive.||||reg||11
Z||000005||TOM||10-06-1999||11:29 PM||||Fieros do have joints. They have CV joints outboard and tripod joints inboard like a front wheel drive car. I dont know how many miles they are good for. I think mine are worn out too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-07-1999||09:12 AM||||Rich,<P>look at the trunk side of the air cleaner, and the coresponding area on the trunk wall. The torque strut I just rebuilt was so soft the air cleaner was actually hitting the trunk wall. Clunk!<P>As you decel the motor shifts forward. when you hit the gas again it shifts aft Real fast. A stock strut can allow 2" plus of travel.<P>Could it be the CV's? yeah. but if you have known soft mounts I'd bet on part of the engine or exhaust hitting the body some place as the motor rocks back an forth. The 2 inches of travel in the torque strut bushings can be even more when it translates to other things connected to the block.<P>A bit of warning tho.... if you put in the urethane bushings you will find out quickly just how rough a 4 cyl really idles, even when it's idleing correctly. All the vibration previously absorbed in the sloppy bushing will go straight to the body.||||reg||1
Z||000007||WGraffiKs||10-07-1999||04:07 PM||||Are there any benefits to replacing the stock bogbone bushings with polyU, other than possibly longer life?  If the PolyU's transmit more of the engine vib. to the frame, that doesn't seem to be a good thing.  (Comment especially directed towards V6's)||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-07-1999||05:13 PM||||at a good idle I don't think the V6 shakes as much as the 4. At least that's my impression. Also, doesn't the V6 bone go to the cradle? I haven't seen one in a while. If it's going to the craddle then the vibration is still isolated in the craddle by its rather huge mounts.<P>The 4 cyl is only rougher on you at idle with the poly bushings. The rest of the time it's not bad. It's a bit anoying, but having the air cleaner banging the body is down right dangerous. Now if I can figure out a way to make the 4 cyl strut go to the craddle I'd be all set.<P>Plus mine is an 87 4 cyl.... these have only 2 mounts for the engine/tranny. You guys with the older setups that have 3+ mounts are stiffer to start with. There is nothing at all keeping the 2 point mount set steady but the strut. I've gone so far as considering changing the thing to the older style mounts, but I just don't have time.<P>As far as life, they Will last longer. The odds of ever having to repalace them again are very small. They are of course stiffer so the engine won't move around as much.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-07-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||10-08-1999||02:04 AM||||The vibrations emitted from a four cylinder,four stroke engine are much,much more than any other.Solid/urethane stuff in a four is very annoying.<BR>There's an engineers handbook,can't find it now,but it shows the sine waves on a graph for comparison,it's about 3-4 times as great as I recall.<BR>Unless you're losing at the dragstrip,stick with new rubber,works just fine.<BR>If the clunking happens with just a slight,on,and off of the throttle,it may well be a CV joint,or several.But if the noise only happens upon a major acceleration/deceleration,that would more likely be a mount/dogbone problem.||||reg||1
Z||000010||SCCA FIERO||10-08-1999||02:21 AM||||Orge, the Fiero dogbone, in both 4 and 6 cylinder form, bolts in at the same point. When I put my 86 V6 in my 88 Coupe, the dogbone bolted right up to the location the 4 cylinder d/b mounted.||||reg||12
